"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[9706],{9706:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-07","slug":"base-07","trackId":"base","order":8,"title":"Loans and Liquidation Risk","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":13,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-07"],"markdown":"Lesson 7 \xb7 9 min\\n\\n## Loans\\n\\nCredit and Borrowing in Cryptocurrency\\n\\nLet us see how these tools work together through one of DeFi’s most common applications: cryptocurrency lending. Suppose you own ETH that you do not want to sell, but need USDC. You can obtain the funds by pledging your cryptocurrency as collateral.\\n\\nAlternatively, you can lend your USDC to other users and earn interest. Smart contracts, discussed in the previous lesson, enable this. The basic lending logic is familiar:\\n- someone lends money;\\n- someone borrows it;\\n- interest is paid for its use.\\n\\nThere is an important difference from a conventional bank. A bank may lend because it knows your identity, income and credit history, which help it assess repayment capacity. DeFi’s main model is different.\\n\\nTo borrow, first provide collateral worth more than the loan.\\n\\nThese are called overcollateralized loans.\\n\\n### 1. Why Take a Crypto Loan?\\n\\nSuppose you hold $10 000 of ETH and do not want to sell it, but need $3 000 in stablecoins. You could sell some ETH, or pledge it and borrow against it.\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\nETH remains your asset, but temporarily secures the loan.\\n\\nYou receive 3 000 USDC to use as you choose. Once you repay the debt and accrued interest, the collateral becomes available again. This is not extra money for free. You incur interest-bearing debt, while your ETH’s price continues changing.\\n\\nIf ETH falls sharply, you risk losing part of the collateral.\\n\\n#### When Must the Loan Be Repaid?\\n\\nUnlike ordinary bank loans, many DeFi services have no compulsory monthly payments or fixed maturity date. You can repay whenever you choose, provided you meet the system’s conditions. Interest continues accruing until repayment.\\n\\nIf collateral value becomes insufficient, however, the system may automatically sell part of it even if you did not intend to repay soon.\\n\\n### 2. Why Pledge More Than You Borrow?\\n\\nWhy pledge $10 000 to borrow only $5 000? Cryptocurrency prices change constantly, so the system needs a buffer against falling collateral values.\\n\\nThink of a pawnshop. You bring a watch worth 100 000 ₽. The shop does not lend the full 100 000 ₽. It might offer 50 000 ₽, leaving a buffer if the watch depreciates.\\n\\nCrypto lending follows similar logic.\\n\\nCollateral must exceed the loan so that the system can recover the money lent even if the asset price moves adversely.\\n\\nBut what happens if collateral falls sharply anyway?\\n\\n### 3. What Is Liquidation?\\n\\nLiquidation is a central risk of crypto lending. Suppose you pledge $10 000 of ETH and borrow $5 000. The service liquidates when debt reaches 80% of collateral value. While ETH is worth $10 000:\\n- Collateral value — $10 000.\\n- Debt — $5 000.\\n- Debt is 50% of collateral value.\\n\\nThe market then falls, reducing your ETH’s value to $6 250. The $5 000 debt is now 80% of collateral value. You have reached the threshold.\\n\\nThe system may automatically sell part of your ETH to repay debt.\\n\\nThis is liquidation. It is not merely a sale at market price. Another participant, the liquidator, sells your collateral. As compensation, the protocol lets them acquire some collateral at a discount, usually 5 to 10%. You bear that discount.\\n\\nLiquidation therefore means selling collateral into a falling market and paying an additional penalty.\\n\\nNo bank employee will call and give you another week. The mechanism can activate automatically at the threshold. Our example ignores accrued interest and fees. In real services, these affect liquidation timing too, and rules depend on the asset and system.\\n\\n#### Health Factor: Measuring Loan Health\\n\\nAave ↗ (opens in a new tab) and similar services display Health Factor, measuring the distance to liquidation:\\n\\nHealth Factor = collateral value \xd7 liquidation threshold / debt\\n\\nWith $10 000 collateral, an 80% threshold and $5 000 debt:\\n\\n10 000 \xd7 0,8 / 5 000 = 1,6\\n\\nThe rule is simple:\\n- above 1: the loan has a safety buffer;\\n- nearer 1: the buffer is smaller;\\n- below 1: liquidation begins.\\n\\nIf ETH’s value falls to $6 250, Health Factor reaches exactly 1.\\n\\nMonitoring Health Factor is convenient because it immediately shows the remaining buffer.\\n\\n### 4. Why You Should Not Borrow the Maximum\\n\\nSuppose the system allows borrowing up to 70% of collateral value. With $10 000 of ETH, the maximum loan is $7 000. This does not mean borrowing the full $7 000 is safe.\\n\\nThe larger the loan relative to collateral, the smaller the market decline needed for liquidation. Borrowing $3 000 leaves substantially more resilience than borrowing $7 000. Experienced users therefore leave a gap between debt and the liquidation threshold. A simple rule:\\n\\nMaximum available loan ≠ safe loan.\\n\\nEven a large buffer is no guarantee: crypto can fall suddenly while loan interest continues increasing debt.\\n\\nCollateral: $10 000 of ETH. How much can you borrow and sleep comfortably? Borrowed $5 000 \xb7 50% of collateral. ETH price unchanged. Health Factor: buffer available, 1,60. Scale 0, 1 — liquidation, 3 +. ETH can decline another 37%. Beyond that: liquidation and a 5–10% penalty.\\n\\n### 5. Who Supplies Loans and Why?\\n\\nWhere does the USDC you borrow come from? Other users supply it. Suppose you have 10 000 USDC sitting in a wallet. You can deposit it into a DeFi service so others can borrow it.\\n\\nThe mechanism is straightforward:\\n- One user supplies USDC.\\n- Another borrows USDC against crypto collateral.\\n- The borrower pays interest.\\n- The supplier receives part of that interest.\\n\\nThe analogy with banking is clear: depositors supply funds, the bank lends, and borrowers pay interest. In DeFi, many of these processes run automatically under predefined rules.\\n\\nThe source of income is identifiable.\\n\\nIt is not money from nowhere. Users needing borrowed funds pay the interest. The service may retain some interest payments under its own rules.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n### 6. Why Interest Rates Keep Changing\\n\\nIn the largest crypto lending systems, rates are generally dynamic, changing with supply and demand. Imagine $100 million of USDC deposited. If most is available and few people want to borrow, rates will be low.\\n\\nIf almost all USDC is borrowed and demand increases further, rates rise. Money has a price here too.\\n\\nPlenty of available funds → cheaper borrowing.\\n\\nFew available funds → more expensive borrowing.\\n\\nA loan costing 4% annually today may cost 5% or more later. The supplier’s return changes too. The rate is not necessarily fixed when the loan is taken. A sharp rise in demand can make servicing your loan more expensive.\\n\\n### 7. Aave and Morpho: Major Lending Services\\n\\nCrypto has many lending services. Aave ↗ (opens in a new tab) and Morpho ↗ (opens in a new tab) are among the largest. Users can supply cryptocurrency for lending or borrow against their own assets. Their size is often measured by TVL, Total Value Locked: the combined value of assets deposited in their smart contracts.\\n\\nAccording to DeFiLlama ↗ (opens in a new tab), on 17 October 2026:\\n- Aave — approximately $17,7 billion TVL.\\n- Morpho — approximately $11 billion TVL.\\n\\nThis places them among the largest DeFi lending participants. TVL does not mean the entire amount has been lent. It measures assets within the system and changes with market prices. Behind simple Deposit and Borrow buttons lies a substantial financial system:\\n- different collateral types;\\n- dynamic interest rates;\\n- liquidation rules;\\n- risk management;\\n- many supported assets;\\n- safeguards against sharp market moves.\\n\\nA foundation course does not require mastering every mechanism. Understand that these services let users interact through smart contracts rather than a traditional bank.\\n\\n### 8. What Are the Risks?\\n\\nSupplying cryptocurrency for loans can look simple. You see a 6% annual USDC yield, deposit funds and earn borrowers’ interest. A clear income source does not mean the funds are safe.\\n\\nCrypto lending has several major risks.\\n\\n1. Smart-contract defects\\n\\nAttackers can exploit a software vulnerability and steal funds.\\n\\n2. Sudden market crashes\\n\\nWhen collateral values fall quickly, the system must liquidate in time. Extreme price moves or liquidation problems can produce losses.\\n\\n3. Insufficient available funds\\n\\nIf most deposited assets are already lent, users may not be able to withdraw the full amount immediately. They may need to wait for loan repayments or new liquidity.\\n\\n4. Asset risks\\n\\nEven stablecoins have no guaranteed value. USDC generally aims to stay near $1, but can temporarily lose its dollar peg in certain circumstances.\\n\\n5. Price-data errors\\n\\nA smart contract does not independently know ETH’s price. An external source, an oracle, supplies it. Incorrect, delayed or manipulated prices can cause unjustified liquidation or prevent timely liquidation. Crypto lending is therefore not simply a higher-rate bank deposit.\\n\\nUnderstanding the income source is only half the task. Understanding how money could be lost is equally important.\\n\\n#### For Deeper Study\\n\\nThe paid course examines Aave ↗ (opens in a new tab) and Morpho ↗ (opens in a new tab), two leading crypto lending projects, in detail: financial mechanisms, project revenue, user risks and methods of evaluating these instruments.\\n\\n### 9. The Essentials\\n\\nCrypto loans let you borrow against cryptocurrency without selling your assets. Overcollateralization means the collateral is worth more than the loan. Liquidation automatically sells some or all required collateral when its value no longer adequately secures the debt. Health Factor measures the distance to liquidation; below 1 means liquidation.\\n\\nRates generally change with supply and demand. Lenders earn by supplying assets to other users, while accepting system risks. Crypto lending creates financial opportunities, but requires understanding loans, collateral and their risks.\\n\\nDo not confuse the ability to borrow or earn interest with a guarantee of financial benefit.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nWhy does a crypto loan usually require collateral worth more than the loan itself?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Credit History** — A record of how you have repaid loans. A poor or missing history can mean rejection or an above-market interest rate. No history is worse than a history of repaid loans\\n- **Liquidation** — The forced sale of collateral after its value falls. In crypto, code simply executes the condition: nobody calls or warns you first\\n- **Smart Contract** — Code that runs automatically when a condition is met, without intermediaries or the option to change your mind. Like a vending machine, but for money and transactions\\n- **Supply and Demand** — The economic law that always works: few goods and many buyers drive prices up; the reverse drives them down\\n- **Stablecoin** — A cryptocurrency pegged one-to-one to the dollar (USDT, USDC). A “dollar flash drive”: transfer it to another country in a minute. More money passes through stablecoins than through Bitcoin\\n- **DeFi (Decentralized Finance)** — An attempt to rebuild the banking system with smart contracts: no banks, offices or employees, only code. It operates 24/7, but without insurance. Both a revolution and a casino\\n- **USDC** — Circle’s stablecoin, considered the most transparent: regular audits and clearly described reserves\\n- **Yield** — The annual percentage an investment actually earns at today’s price. A bond’s price and yield move in opposite directions: when the bond becomes cheaper, its yield rises. Government bond yields influence loan and deposit rates\\n- **Health Factor** — A crypto loan’s health measure: collateral value \xd7 liquidation threshold / debt. Above 1 means a buffer; approaching 1 means the buffer is shrinking; below 1 triggers liquidation\\n- **Overcollateralized Loan** — A loan whose collateral is worth more than the debt: deposit $10 000 of ETH and borrow $5 000. The buffer is necessary because collateral prices change constantly\\n- **Oracle (in DeFi)** — An external source reporting prices to a smart contract. An error or manipulation can cause the contract to liquidate collateral when it should not\\n- **TVL (Total Value Locked)** — The amount of assets deposited in a service’s smart contracts. It indicates size, not how much has been lent or how safe the service is\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: Why does a crypto loan usually require collateral worth more than the loan itself?**\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 1: To give the system a buffer against falling collateral value\\n- ⚪ Option 2: Because the service always keeps half the collateral\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Because crypto loans are only available to wealthy users\\n\\n**Question 2: What happens if collateral value falls sharply and no longer adequately secures the debt?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: The debt is automatically forgiven\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Some or all of the necessary collateral may be liquidated\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Interest stops accruing\\n\\n**Question 3: Why can a crypto loan’s interest rate change?**\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 1: It generally depends on demand and the amount of available funds\\n- ⚪ Option 2: The exchange chooses it randomly each day\\n- ⚪ Option 3: It is always fixed forever when the first loan is taken\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-07-1","prompt":"Why does a crypto loan usually require collateral worth more than the loan itself?","options":[{"id":"base-07-1-1","label":"To give the system a buffer against falling collateral value","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. To give the system a buffer against falling collateral value"},{"id":"base-07-1-2","label":"Because the service always keeps half the collateral","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. To give the system a buffer against falling collateral value"},{"id":"base-07-1-3","label":"Because crypto loans are only available to wealthy users","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. To give the system a buffer against falling collateral value"}],"explanation":"To give the system a buffer against falling collateral value","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1512–1515 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-07-2","prompt":"What happens if collateral value falls sharply and no longer adequately secures the debt?","options":[{"id":"base-07-2-1","label":"The debt is automatically forgiven","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Some or all of the necessary collateral may be liquidated"},{"id":"base-07-2-2","label":"Some or all of the necessary collateral may be liquidated","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Some or all of the necessary collateral may be liquidated"},{"id":"base-07-2-3","label":"Interest stops accruing","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Some or all of the necessary collateral may be liquidated"}],"explanation":"Some or all of the necessary collateral may be liquidated","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1517–1520 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-07-3","prompt":"Why can a crypto loan’s interest rate change?","options":[{"id":"base-07-3-1","label":"It generally depends on demand and the amount of available funds","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. It generally depends on demand and the amount of available funds"},{"id":"base-07-3-2","label":"The exchange chooses it randomly each day","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. It generally depends on demand and the amount of available funds"},{"id":"base-07-3-3","label":"It is always fixed forever when the first loan is taken","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. It generally depends on demand and the amount of available funds"}],"explanation":"It generally depends on demand and the amount of available funds","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:1522–1525 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":1301,"lineEnd":1530}}')}}]);